.DS_Store
dist/
build/
.next/
# Auto-generated by `next dev` and `next build`, which both recreate it if
# missing; `tsc` alone cannot, so run either before relying on it. Its contents
# are machine- and mode-dependent: `next dev` writes `.next/dev/types/...`
# while `next build` writes `.next/types/...`, and a Next upgrade adds or drops
# further imports. Every path it references lives under the gitignored
# `.next/`, so a committed copy was stale on a fresh clone by construction.
# Tracking it meant the tree went dirty on every dev/build switch and every
# dependency bump.
next-env.d.ts
.vercel/
*.log
coverage/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo
PAT.txt
GITHUB_PUSH.md
.agent
.gemini/
.gemini_security/
# JetBrains project files: personal IDE state that changes with the folder
# name and belongs to whoever runs the IDE, not to the repository.
.idea/
graphify-out/

# Local agent/tooling state - not part of the app (matches the Capytools convention:
# plans, research and session scratch stay on the machine, never in the repo).
.hermes/
.opencode/
dogfood-output/
.mimosa/

# `npx skills add` writes the skills to `.agents/skills/` (the copy we commit,
# pinned by skills-lock.json) and then symlinks them into `.claude/skills/` for
# Claude Code. That second tree is only a shim, and git here runs with
# `core.symlinks=false`, so committing it would dereference every link and store
# a duplicate of the whole payload. Use `npx skills experimental_install` to
# recreate both trees from skills-lock.json.
.claude/

# Local-only recovery bundles. These are git bundles containing branch history
# that is deliberately not on the remote; they are tens of megabytes and are
# machine-specific, so they must never be committed.
backups/